# The physical keyboard that gets bridged to the host.
#
# Two things happen here: a stable symlink, and a systemd dependency that
# starts the bridge when the keyboard appears and stops it when the
# keyboard is unplugged. No polling anywhere.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE VENDOR ID BELOW IS A DEFAULT AND MUST BE CONFIRMED ON YOUR BOARD.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 04d9 is Holtek, which is what the official Raspberry Pi keyboard
# reports. A different keyboard has a different id, and a rule that
# matches nothing is not an error: udev creates no symlink, the device
# unit never appears, the bridge never starts, and nothing anywhere says
# why. Confirm before trusting it:
#
#     lsusb
#     udevadm info -a -n /dev/input/eventN | grep -m1 idVendor
#     bench-gadget status
#
# WHAT THIS RULE COSTS WHEN IT MATCHES THE WRONG KEYBOARD. The bridge
# grabs whatever it is pointed at, and a grabbed keyboard is invisible to
# the Pi itself. If you plug a keyboard into this board intending to type
# on the Pi and this rule matches it, that keyboard will type on the HOST
# instead and the Pi will appear to ignore it. The way back is the
# USB/TTL serial console, which is why that cable is not optional in this
# project.
#
# ENV{ID_INPUT_KEYBOARD}=="1" is what keeps this from matching the mouse
# and the consumer-control interface that most keyboards also present.
# Without it a composite keyboard produces two or three event devices and
# the symlink lands on whichever enumerated last.

ACTION=="add|change", SUBSYSTEM=="input", KERNEL=="event*", \
    ENV{ID_INPUT_KEYBOARD}=="1", ATTRS{idVendor}=="04d9", \
    SYMLINK+="input/bench-kbd", \
    TAG+="systemd", ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}="kbd-bridge.service"
